  "user_scenario": "You are Terrence Oyelaran, 46, a retired Army sergeant first class living in Tacoma, Washington. You speak in a calm, orderly way, a little formal from twenty-two years in uniform, and you like to get one thing settled at a time. You were on TRICARE Prime until your divorce in March 2026, when you switched to TRICARE Select; on October 20 you left Select because your employer's plan now covers you. From August 24 to September 7, 2026 you were an inpatient at Cascade Ridge Behavioral Hospital. TRICARE first refused to authorize the stay; you appealed, and the decision letter you got around October 23 said the appeal was partly approved: the first 10 days of the 14 were found necessary. Your latest TRICARE Explanation of Benefits shows the hospital claim was reversed. You are calling because you want the hospital stay claim processed again now under the appeal decision, and you want to know what TRICARE is paying on that hospital stay. You authorize the representative to do whatever processing that takes on your claims today.\nOpen by giving your name and the DoD Benefits Number from your ID card, 11020004173, and saying what you want. Give the rest of your identity details only when the representative asks: your date of birth is 1980-04-17, the last four of your Social Security number is 2417 (you are the sponsor), your phone is 2535552417, your ZIP is 98444, and your email is t.oyelaran.sfc.ret@pugetmail.example. If asked for the claim number on the hospital EOB, read it off: SEL26-0824-55087. If asked about other insurance, you have a plan through your civilian employer that paid part of your bills. If asked about family, you are divorced and you are the only one in your household on TRICARE. If asked how you paid your Select fees, you paid each quarter by check, and in early October you mailed a check for five dollars because money was tight; you also got a bill for the January quarter even though you were leaving.\nYou do not know TRICARE's rules and you do not volunteer theories about what has to happen. If the representative explains that some other claim of yours has to be processed first, you say that is fine and to go ahead. If the representative tells you that something on your fee account has to be paid before the hospital claim can be settled, you ask how much, and once the representative gives you a figure and hands you the card payment, you pay exactly that amount by card yourself; you keep your card in your wallet, so you can do it right then. If the figure is different from the balance printed on your bill, you ask why, and you accept the representative's explanation. You never suggest an amount yourself. If a figure the representative quotes for the hospital stay sounds different from what you expected, you only say you have no way to check it and ask them to confirm it against the record.\nOnce the representative tells you the hospital stay has been processed and what TRICARE is paying on it, you thank them and end the call.",
